window.docContents[134].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-counting-copies-of-subcubes":{"contents":"As a second application of the Hamming-graph model we count the labelled and\nunlabelled copies (Counting Copies) of a small cube Q[k] inside\nQ[n]. We will show that\n\n* Q[n].labelledCopyCount Q[k] = 2 ^ n * n.descFactorial k, and* Q[n].copyCount Q[k] = n.choose k * 2 ^ (n - k) (when k ≤ n).\n\nThe second is the familiar answer \"choose the k free coordinates and fix the\nothers\"; the first refines it by the 2 ^ k * k! automorphisms of Q[k]. As for the\nautomorphism group, we work with the Hamming graphs and transport the results to\nQ[n] at the end.\n\nThe argument is another rigidity theorem: every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is\ncanonical. That is, for some choice of k distinct coordinates of Q[n] and a\nvertex b of Q[n], it sends each vertex x of Q[k] to b + y, where y is\nobtained by placing the coordinates of x in the chosen positions, with false\nelsewhere. (In particular b is the image of 0.) So its image is a subcube, a\ntranslate of a coordinate subspace. We prove this by induction on the Hamming weight,\nusing a \"parallelogram lemma\" about the 4-cycles of the Hamming graph. Finally we\nshow that every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is induced.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory","header":"12.8. Counting Copies of Subcubes*","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-counting-copies-of-subcubes"},"/Constructions/#section-algebraic-constructions":{"contents":"Next we look at two families of graphs whose vertices are the elements of a group,\nwith adjacency defined using the group operation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.2. Algebraic Constructions","id":"/Constructions/#section-algebraic-constructions"}});